## C++ Applications And Binaries

Run these commands from the directory containing this project's `CCGO.toml`:

```bash
ccgo run --app basic
ccgo run --app basic -- --help
ccgo run --app basic --release --build-only
```

The starter in `app/basic/main.cpp` is a standalone C++ program. Applications
use `[[app]]`; executable tools use `[[bin]]` and `ccgo run --bin <name>`.
Unconfigured C++ apps can be discovered as `app/<name>.cpp`, `.cc`, `.cxx`,
or `app/<name>/main.cpp`, `main.cc`, `main.cxx`.
These commands build for the local host, with app outputs under `target/run/app/`.
The platform application in `app/kmp/` uses its own Gradle/Xcode tooling.

## Desktop And UI Applications

```bash
ccgo run --app imgui-demo
ccgo run --app imgui-demo --release --build-only
```

The ImGui starter in `app/imgui/` uses CMake, GLFW and OpenGL; its first
build downloads pinned dependency versions. Enter text and press **Call SDK** to
call `Echo` and display `{{ cpy_project_name }}: <input>`. CMake links the project's
`src/api/native/echo.cc` as a separate static library using its public header.

Use `[[app]]` for a C++/CMake/ImGui/Qt, Flutter or Tauri project in `app/`.
Flutter calls `flutter run`/`flutter build`; Tauri calls `cargo tauri dev`/`build`.
Install the toolchain and app dependencies first. Apps keep their own build
configuration; CMake apps build under `target/run/app/`.

